; Plan 04's RULE 2, second branch, over a lane that was rendered on TWO
; MIDI channels and edited on ONE.
;
; The document behind this fixture has one notes lane, `kit`, whose two
; notes name `:ch 1` and `:ch 2`. `tempura build` renders such a lane
; onto ONE track and writes ONE LANE RECORD PER CHANNEL, because a lane
; hash is over a `(track, ch)` pair (docs/residue.md, "The hashes"). So
; the file carries two records for `kit`, both naming track 2.
;
; `tools/gen_fixtures.zig` then re-velocities the channel-2 note the way
; a DAW would and writes the file back with the residue untouched.
; Channel 2's record is stale; channel 1's still matches.
;
; The lane is ONE thing, so it binds — and it binds at every address its
; records name: `:from song :track 2 :ch [1 2]`. Until this case existed a
; dump bound the lane at the stale record's channel alone, and channel
; 1's rendered notes stayed in the tier belonging to no lane: gone from
; every timeline query and from the score. The verdict table shows one
; line PER RECORD, both `became_bound` — the lane's verdict — each with
; its own `:hash-ok`, and the `(consumed …)` line counts only the
; conductor's events: a bound lane's channels stay in the tier for it to
; read, the untouched one included.
;
; `(export-score)` is the proof that both channels reach the score: two
; triggers, one per note, on a lane that reads two channels.
(dump)
(export-score)
